# Phase 24 - Interaction Feel, Motion, Focus, Scroll, Touch, and Accessibility Audit

Date: 2026-06-26
Scope: press behavior, button primitives, icon buttons, focus visibility, input focus stability, mobile viewport/keyboard handling, scroll lock, overlay stability, motion keyframes, ARIA/live-state patterns.
Goal lens: refined, breathable, simple. A top-tier product should not only look clean; it should feel calm, predictable, and physically stable.
Benchmark lens: X, Threads, Instagram, and Apple-style interaction principles: immediate but not twitchy, few motion patterns, clear focus, stable keyboard behavior, no accidental scroll jumps, and accessible recovery states.
Evidence type: static source and interaction-contract audit. This phase does not claim runtime device validation.

## Files inspected

- `src/styles/system/ui-primitives-interactions.css`
- `src/styles/utilities/motion-scroll.css`
- `src/styles/system/ui-input-focus-stability-contract.css`
- `src/styles/system/mobile-viewport-contract.css`
- `src/styles/utilities/mobile-overlay-stability.css`
- `src/styles/components/buttons.css`
- `src/ui/ActionButton.tsx`
- `src/ui/IconButton.tsx`
- `src/ui/TopbarIconButton.tsx`
- `src/ui/PublishIconButton.tsx`
- `src/ui/ProfileIconButton.tsx`
- `src/hooks/useInstantPress.ts`
- `src/utils/scrollLock.ts`
- `src/hooks/useMobileAddressBar.ts`
- `src/hooks/useFocusScrollStabilizer.ts`
- `src/utils/postCreateFocusBridge.ts`
- Static interaction evidence from `src/ui`, `src/hooks`, `src/utils`, and `src/styles`.

## Executive summary

The app has a stronger interaction foundation than the average frontend. There are explicit contracts for press feedback, mobile viewport variables, input focus stability, route overlay scroll behavior, bottom nav focus, ARIA loading states, and scroll locking. These are exactly the kinds of details that make a mobile social product feel polished.

The maturity gap is that the interaction system is not fully centralized. Some components use `ActionButton` and semantic classes; others still build interaction directly with `pressable`, `inline-flex`, `transition`, `active:opacity-95`, `!p-0`, raw icon sizes, and custom timing constants. Input focus stability is intentionally suppressing outlines to avoid layout jump, but that creates an accessibility tradeoff that needs a parallel visible-focus contract for keyboard users.

The next step should be an `Interaction Contract` that owns press timing, focus visibility, touch targets, icon sizing, motion names, keyboard viewport behavior, and async action states.

## Overall health

Score: 7.2 / 10

Strengths:

- `pressable` centralizes tap highlight, user-select, transform origin, and transitions in `src/styles/utilities/motion-scroll.css:4-14`.
- Input focus stability is explicitly documented as a contract in `src/styles/system/ui-input-focus-stability-contract.css:1-14`.
- Route overlays avoid transform and use viewport-fixed height/scroll containment in `src/styles/utilities/mobile-overlay-stability.css:1-21`.
- Mobile viewport variables track layout height, visual height, visual offset, and keyboard inset through `src/hooks/useMobileAddressBar.ts` and `src/styles/system/mobile-viewport-contract.css:3-13`.
- Scroll lock freezes visible scroll targets rather than relying only on fixed body positioning in `src/utils/scrollLock.ts:1-259`.
- `ActionButton` maps variant/size to semantic classes and exposes `data-action-state` in `src/ui/ActionButton.tsx:29-78`.
- Loading and refresh patterns expose ARIA live/busy behavior in `src/ui/LoadingState.tsx`.

Primary risks:

- Press behavior is split between CSS `.pressable`, `useInstantPress`, and ordinary `onClick` buttons.
- Some global icon buttons still compose layout utility strings and raw icon sizes in TSX.
- Focus stabilization suppresses outlines broadly without a fully visible keyboard-focus replacement everywhere.
- Motion keyframes exist globally, but components still use arbitrary animation classes.
- Mobile keyboard and scroll stabilization include raw timing values that are not governed by interaction tokens.

## P0 findings

### P0-1 - Press behavior has two systems: CSS pressable and JS instant press

Evidence:

- `.pressable` defines visual press transitions in `src/styles/utilities/motion-scroll.css:4-14`.
- `.pressable:active` currently sets `transform: none` and shortens transition duration in `src/styles/utilities/motion-scroll.css:25-30`.
- `useInstantPress` fires on `pointerup`/`touchend`, suppresses follow-up click, and dedupes with `PRESS_DEDUPE_MS = 320` in `src/hooks/useInstantPress.ts:14-129`.
- Some key controls use `useInstantPress`, such as `TopbarIconButton`, `PublishIconButton`, and home tabs.
- Many other controls use plain `pressable` plus normal click handlers, especially admin and feature buttons.

Why this hurts refinement:

- Some buttons respond immediately through the hook; others wait for normal click timing.
- Some controls animate via CSS active state; others deliberately do not visually transform.
- A top-tier product needs consistent perceived latency across nav, tabs, cards, CTA buttons, and icon controls.

Recommendation:

- Define an `InteractionPress Contract` with three layers:
  - `visualPress`: CSS feedback for hover/active/focus.
  - `instantPress`: JS dedupe/tap latency behavior for high-frequency mobile navigation.
  - `asyncPress`: disabled/busy/cooldown behavior for network actions.
- Expose this through component primitives instead of ad hoc `pressable` use.
- Tokenize `PRESS_DEDUPE_MS` as an interaction timing token.

Acceptance gate:

- All primary nav, topbar, tab, card, and CTA interactions declare whether they are `instant`, `normal`, or `async`.
- New components do not use raw `pressable` without a higher-level interaction primitive.

### P0-2 - Focus visibility is intentionally stable, but keyboard focus needs a stronger visible replacement

Evidence:

- Input focus contract sets focus border, box-shadow, outline, and transform to none in `src/styles/system/ui-input-focus-stability-contract.css:8-31`.
- `.pressable:focus`, `.pressable:focus-visible`, and `.pressable:active` remove box shadow in `src/styles/utilities/motion-scroll.css:32-36`.
- Some components do define focus-visible styles, such as bottom nav in `src/styles/components/bottom-nav.css`, feed card actions, telegram contact action, and post tag links.
- Several TSX components also use `focus:outline-none` or rely on classes that may suppress visible focus.

Why this hurts refinement and accessibility:

- The no-jump input rule is good for mobile polish.
- But keyboard users still need a clear visible focus state on inputs, buttons, tabs, cards, and links.
- Removing focus visuals globally creates a risk that some controls become invisible during keyboard navigation.

Recommendation:

- Split focus policy into two contracts:
  - `FocusStability`: no layout shift, no zoom, no extra border growth.
  - `FocusVisibility`: visible ring/surface for keyboard focus only.
- Use `:focus-visible` with non-layout-affecting outline/box-shadow or pseudo-element rings.
- Audit every interactive primitive for a visible focus state.

Acceptance gate:

- Every interactive primitive has a visible `:focus-visible` state.
- Inputs keep no layout shift but still expose a visible focus affordance for keyboard navigation.

### P0-3 - Icon/button sizing still leaks through TSX

Evidence:

- `PublishIconButton` returns raw icon sizes `24` and `19`, and raw stroke widths `2.25` and `2.8` in `src/ui/PublishIconButton.tsx:27-35`.
- `PublishIconButton` includes utility classes and `active:opacity-95` in `src/ui/PublishIconButton.tsx:16-24`.
- `PublishIconButton` wraps the icon in `inline-flex h-full w-full items-center justify-center` in `src/ui/PublishIconButton.tsx:68-75`.
- `TopbarIconButton` includes `inline-flex items-center justify-center rounded-full transition` in `src/ui/TopbarIconButton.tsx:49-53`.
- `ProfileIconButton` includes `inline-flex items-center justify-center`, `!p-0`, `transition`, and `ui-bg-card` in `src/ui/ProfileIconButton.tsx:24-39`.
- `LoadingState` uses raw spinner/icon sizes `18`, `22`, and `14` in `src/ui/LoadingState.tsx`.

Why this hurts refinement:

- Icons are one of the most visible pixel-level cues. Small inconsistencies in icon size/stroke make the UI feel slightly off.
- TSX-owned dimensions make it hard to tune touch targets and optical balance across the whole app.

Recommendation:

- Create an `IconSize Contract`: `xs`, `sm`, `md`, `lg`, `topbar`, `publish`, `spinner`, `status`.
- Create semantic classes for icon wrappers: `ui-icon-button-content`, `ui-publish-icon`, `ui-profile-avatar-button`.
- Avoid `!p-0`, `active:*`, and raw inline icon dimensions in reusable components.

Acceptance gate:

- Reusable UI components do not pass raw numeric icon sizes except through an approved escape hatch.
- Icon buttons use semantic content/wrapper classes only.

### P0-4 - Motion keyframes are centralized, but usage still escapes through arbitrary animation classes

Evidence:

- Global motion keyframes exist: `fadeIn`, `toast-in`, `feed-item-in`, `sheet-pop-in`, `sheet-up-in`, `tab-refresh-dot-bounce`, `tab-refresh-dot-swing` in `src/styles/utilities/motion-scroll.css:143-250`.
- Phase 23 found AuthModal using `animate-[fadeIn_160ms_ease-out]`, `animate-[sheet-pop-in_180ms_ease-out]`, and `animate-[fadeIn_140ms_ease-out]`.
- Promote payment sheet also previously showed arbitrary animation usage.
- Some feedback animations use hardcoded durations, such as feed like pop at `260ms` and follow pending sheen at `900ms` from static evidence.

Why this hurts refinement:

- Motion should feel like one product, not a set of similar local decisions.
- Arbitrary animation classes make reduced-motion support and timing tuning harder.

Recommendation:

- Introduce named motion utility classes: `ui-motion-fade-in`, `ui-motion-sheet-pop-in`, `ui-motion-sheet-up-in`, `ui-motion-feedback-pop`, `ui-motion-pending-sheen`.
- Map all durations to motion tokens.
- Ensure every motion class has a `prefers-reduced-motion` behavior.

Acceptance gate:

- No production TSX uses `animate-[...]` for app UI motion.
- Motion names, durations, easing, and reduced-motion behavior live in one contract.

### P0-5 - Keyboard and viewport stabilization are robust, but timing values are scattered

Evidence:

- `useMobileAddressBar` tracks mobile width, visual viewport scroll noise, text entry state, blur timers, pointerdown intent, focus in/out, visual viewport resize, and keyboard inset.
- It uses local constants and timeouts such as mobile width `1024`, noise threshold `4`, legacy cleanup `80`, blur follow-up `360`, text-entry intent `240`.
- `useFocusScrollStabilizer` uses read CSS tokens for topbar/spacing but also schedules raw delays `[180, 360]` and blur release `120` in `src/hooks/useFocusScrollStabilizer.ts:67-83`.
- `postCreateFocusBridge` uses focus intent TTL `5000`, bridge release `160`, 1px hidden textarea dimensions, `16px` font/line-height, and z-index `2147483647` in `src/utils/postCreateFocusBridge.ts:1-75`.

Why this hurts refinement:

- The engineering is thoughtful, but the numbers are interaction design decisions.
- Without a named contract, tuning keyboard behavior can become trial-and-error.
- The focus bridge is a necessary mobile workaround, but its hardcoded visual/invisible geometry should be documented as an engine workaround.

Recommendation:

- Create `interactionTiming.ts` or a CSS/TS shared token map for keyboard, focus, instant press, scroll restore, route warmup, and bridge release timings.
- Document mobile engine workarounds separately from product motion.
- Replace magic z-index/focus bridge geometry with named constants and comments explaining platform necessity.

Acceptance gate:

- Keyboard/focus timing constants are centralized and named by behavior.
- Workaround constants are documented as platform compatibility, not product design values.

## P1 findings

### P1-1 - ActionButton has variants and sizes, but lacks async/loading semantics

Evidence:

- `ActionButton` exposes `data-action-state`, `disabled`, and `aria-busy` passthrough in `src/ui/ActionButton.tsx:9-78`.
- Many pages still place spinner/text composition inside button content manually.
- `useAsyncFlow` and `useActionLock` exist in the codebase, but action-state visuals are not unified at the button primitive level.

Recommendation:

- Add explicit `loading`, `loadingLabel`, `icon`, `iconPosition`, and `fullWidth` props to `ActionButton`, or create an `AsyncActionButton` wrapper.
- Button should own spinner placement, gap, aria-busy, disabled state, and loading copy rhythm.

Acceptance gate:

- Pages do not hand-build `inline-flex items-center gap-*` loading content inside primary buttons.

### P1-2 - Segment tabs are semantic, but TSX still controls visual gaps/text tone in some places

Evidence:

- `ui-segment-tabs` and `ui-segment-tab` are defined in `src/styles/system/ui-primitives-interactions.css:15-87`.
- Static evidence shows `SegmentTabs.tsx` still using utility classes like `flex flex-col items-center`, `gap-0.5`, `gap-0`, `x-caption`, `ui-text-strong`, and `ui-text-subtle`.

Recommendation:

- Let `SegmentTabs` output semantic classes only.
- Use data attributes for label mode, active state, and density.

Acceptance gate:

- Tab visual structure is fully CSS-owned.

### P1-3 - Scroll locking is strong, but allowed scroll lanes need audit tooling

Evidence:

- `scrollLock.ts` supports multiple locks and `allowTouchMove` selectors.
- It freezes visible scroll targets and restores scroll positions.
- It depends on callers passing correct allowed scroll regions, such as auth scroll or route overlay scroll.

Recommendation:

- Add an audit checklist for every overlay/sheet/dialog: which element scrolls, which element is locked, which nested scrolls are allowed.
- Add optional development logging or data attributes for scroll lock ownership.

Acceptance gate:

- Every overlay/sheet has a named allowed scroll surface and no background scroll leak on iOS/Android.

### P1-4 - Touch targets are defined but not enforced universally

Evidence:

- `.tap-target` sets min width/height to `--ui-control-hit` in `src/styles/components/buttons.css:3-7`.
- `ActionButton`, `IconButton`, topbar controls, bottom nav, and many feature controls use different classes and sizes.
- Admin and feature utility buttons frequently set `h-7`, `h-8`, `h-9`, `h-10`, `h-11` in TSX.

Recommendation:

- Define touch target roles: `compact-admin`, `standard`, `topbar`, `bottom-nav`, `inline-chip`.
- On coarse pointers, enforce minimum hit target even when visual size is compact.
- Document when visual compact controls may have larger invisible hit area.

Acceptance gate:

- Coarse-pointer controls meet the app’s hit target minimum unless documented as non-primary inline text controls.

## P2 findings

### P2-1 - Pull refresh and loading micro-motion should share one feedback taxonomy

Evidence:

- Pull refresh uses mark/icon/spinner classes and keyframes in `src/styles/utilities/motion-scroll.css:44-141`.
- Loading spinner and refresh hint live separately in `LoadingState`.
- Tab refresh dots have separate keyframes and timing tokens.

Recommendation:

- Define feedback motion categories: `spinner`, `pulse`, `refresh-dots`, `pending-sheen`, `success-pop`.
- Each category should have duration, easing, reduced-motion behavior, and usage guidance.

### P2-2 - Image/lightbox and media gestures should be included in runtime validation

Evidence:

- Static search shows ImageLightbox has drag/transition logic and raw transition string.
- Media carousel CSS has touch-action, scale feedback, and focus-visible states.

Recommendation:

- Include media gestures in the eventual runtime validation pass: swipe, drag cancel, close, keyboard escape, focus return.

## Recommended contracts

### InteractionPress Contract

Required owners:

- Press visual feedback.
- Instant press dedupe timing.
- Click/touch/pointer event behavior.
- Disabled and busy suppression.
- Coarse pointer hit target.

### FocusVisibility Contract

Required owners:

- Keyboard focus ring/surface.
- Input focus without layout shift.
- Card/link/button/tab focus states.
- Focus trap requirements for dialogs/sheets/lightbox.

### Motion Contract

Required owners:

- Named animation classes.
- Duration tokens.
- Easing tokens.
- Reduced-motion fallbacks.
- Feedback taxonomy.

### MobileViewport Contract

Required owners:

- Visual viewport height.
- Keyboard inset.
- Address bar changes.
- Text-entry active state.
- Scroll restoration after focus.
- Platform workaround constants.

### IconInteraction Contract

Required owners:

- Icon visual sizes.
- Stroke widths.
- Wrapper size.
- Hit target size.
- Badge/online dot placement.

## Implementation sequence

1. Centralize interaction timing constants for instant press, focus stabilization, keyboard intent, bridge release, route restore, and loading feedback.
2. Replace arbitrary animation classes with named motion utility classes.
3. Add semantic icon-size/stroke tokens and migrate `PublishIconButton`, `TopbarIconButton`, `ProfileIconButton`, and loading spinners.
4. Split input focus stability from visible keyboard focus and audit all interactive primitives.
5. Add async/loading semantics to `ActionButton` or create `AsyncActionButton`.
6. Make `SegmentTabs` output semantic classes/data attributes only.
7. Create touch target roles and coarse-pointer enforcement rules.
8. Add runtime validation scenarios for keyboard, overlays, bottom nav, lightbox, media carousel, and mobile text entry.

## Release-readiness gates

Static gates:

- No reusable UI component contains raw visual utility stacks for icon/button alignment.
- No production TSX uses `animate-[...]` for product motion.
- Icon size/stroke values come from semantic tokens or typed size props.
- Interaction timing constants are centralized and named.
- Every interactive primitive has a visible `:focus-visible` state.

Runtime gates:

- Keyboard can navigate topbar, bottom nav, feed cards, tabs, modal controls, sheets, and 404/recovery actions with visible focus.
- Mobile keyboard does not cause post-create, auth, profile, chat, or promote inputs to jump or hide under fixed bars.
- Route overlays and sheets do not leak background scroll.
- Press behavior feels consistent across bottom nav, tabs, cards, action buttons, and icon buttons.
- Reduced-motion users do not receive nonessential motion loops or pop animations.

Accessibility gates:

- `aria-current`, `aria-selected`, `aria-pressed`, `aria-busy`, and `aria-live` usage is consistent with the control pattern.
- Buttons with loading state expose busy/disabled semantics and do not announce duplicate labels.
- Dialogs/sheets trap focus and restore focus to the opener.
- Touch targets meet minimum hit area on coarse pointer devices.

## Current-state conclusion

The product already has many of the right interaction ingredients. The hard work around mobile viewport, input focus stability, route overlay containment, scroll lock, and instant press has clearly begun.

The remaining gap is consolidation. To reach a truly premium Apple-like feel, interaction behavior must stop being partly local. Press, focus, icon sizing, async button loading, motion names, keyboard timing, and touch targets need the same level of governance that the visual token system is starting to get. Once those contracts are enforced, the app can feel not just cleaner, but calmer and more physically reliable.
